<BaseLayout title="About">
    <h1>About Hana Loaf</h1>
    <h2>Our Story</h2>
    <p>
      Hana means flower in Japanese and that single word captures everything
      this bakery stands for. Something that starts small, grows with patience,
      and blooms into something worth stopping for.
    </p>
    <p>
      Hana Loaf was born from a collision of two great bread cultures. Japan
      taught us softness the pillowy milk breads, the delicate sweetness, the
      obsessive attention to texture that Japanese baking is famous for. France
      taught us depth the wild levain, the blistered crust, the slow
      fermentation that turns simple flour and water into something extraordinary.
    </p>
    <p>
      We couldn't choose between the two. So we didn't. Every bake at Hana Loaf
      lives somewhere in that space the softness of the East meeting the
      boldness of the West, sitting together on a shelf in Cape Town, still warm
      from the oven.
    </p>
      <h2>How We Bake</h2>
    <p>
      Everything at Hana Loaf is made from scratch each morning. We use heritage
      grain sourced from local South African mills, pure cultured butter, and
      seasonal fruit from the Western Cape. Our sourdoughs ferment for a minimum
      of 36 hours we never rush the process because you can taste the difference.
    </p>

    <h2>Our Values</h2>
    <p>
      We source locally wherever we can. We donate day-old bread to the Cape Town
      Hunger Project every week. We run free baking workshops at local schools each
      term. Good bread should do more than just taste good it should give back to
      the community that supports it.
    </p>

    <h2>Come Visit Us</h2>
    <p>
      We are open Monday to Saturday from 7am and Sundays from 8am. You'll find us
      at 12 Blossom Lane in De Waterkant. Pull up a chair, order a flat white, and
      stay a while that's what Hana Loaf is for.
    </p>
</BaseLayout>